1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,212
This is Venice.

2
00:00:01,212 --> 00:00:02,651
A city of palaces,

3
00:00:02,651 --> 00:00:04,317
churches and squares,

4
00:00:04,317 --> 00:00:06,740
built in the middle of the sea.

5
00:00:07,090 --> 00:00:08,493
There are no cars.

6
00:00:08,493 --> 00:00:10,265
The streets are canals,

7
00:00:10,265 --> 00:00:11,816
the buses are boats,

8
00:00:11,816 --> 00:00:14,843
and many houses rise straight out of the

9
00:00:14,843 --> 00:00:15,360
water.

10
00:00:15,710 --> 00:00:17,564
And under all that stone,

11
00:00:17,564 --> 00:00:19,489
holding the whole city up,

12
00:00:19,489 --> 00:00:22,126
is something you would never expect:

13
00:00:22,126 --> 00:00:23,980
millions of wooden poles.

14
00:00:24,330 --> 00:00:26,799
Building on land would have been so much

15
00:00:26,799 --> 00:00:27,281
easier.

16
00:00:27,281 --> 00:00:29,448
So why would anyone build a city on

17
00:00:29,448 --> 00:00:29,870
water?

18
00:00:32,820 --> 00:00:34,626
By the end of this video,

19
00:00:34,626 --> 00:00:36,085
you'll see how fear,

20
00:00:36,085 --> 00:00:36,501
salt,

21
00:00:36,501 --> 00:00:39,210
wood and trade turned a swamp into the

22
00:00:39,210 --> 00:00:40,877
richest city in Europe,

23
00:00:40,877 --> 00:00:43,656
and why that city is still fighting the

24
00:00:43,656 --> 00:00:44,420
sea today.

25
00:00:44,770 --> 00:00:47,440
Venice sits at the top of the Adriatic

26
00:00:47,440 --> 00:00:47,782
Sea,

27
00:00:47,782 --> 00:00:49,698
in the north-east of Italy.

28
00:00:49,698 --> 00:00:52,779
Rivers flowing down from the Alps have built

29
00:00:52,779 --> 00:00:53,326
a wide,

30
00:00:53,326 --> 00:00:54,490
flat coast here.

31
00:00:54,840 --> 00:00:57,867
Between that coast and the open sea lies

32
00:00:57,867 --> 00:00:58,458
a huge,

33
00:00:58,458 --> 00:00:59,639
shallow lagoon,

34
00:00:59,639 --> 00:01:01,780
about fifty kilometers long.

35
00:01:01,780 --> 00:01:05,250
It's the largest wetland in the Mediterranean.

36
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:08,900
A long line of narrow sandy islands separates

37
00:01:08,900 --> 00:01:10,694
the lagoon from the sea.

38
00:01:10,694 --> 00:01:13,492
The tide flows in and out through just

39
00:01:13,492 --> 00:01:14,640
three openings.

40
00:01:14,990 --> 00:01:17,401
The city itself is built on around one

41
00:01:17,401 --> 00:01:19,626
hundred and eighteen small islands,

42
00:01:19,626 --> 00:01:22,593
packed so closely together that from above they

43
00:01:22,593 --> 00:01:23,520
look like one.

44
00:01:23,870 --> 00:01:27,010
Those islands are separated by around one hundred

45
00:01:27,010 --> 00:01:28,141
and fifty canals,

46
00:01:28,141 --> 00:01:31,030
and joined by more than four hundred bridges.

47
00:01:31,380 --> 00:01:32,773
The biggest canal,

48
00:01:32,773 --> 00:01:34,020
the Grand Canal,

49
00:01:34,020 --> 00:01:37,027
snakes through the middle of the city in

50
00:01:37,027 --> 00:01:37,833
a giant S,

51
00:01:37,833 --> 00:01:39,887
about four kilometers long.

52
00:01:39,887 --> 00:01:40,987
For centuries,

53
00:01:40,987 --> 00:01:43,040
only one bridge crossed it:

54
00:01:43,040 --> 00:01:43,920
the Rialto.

55
00:01:44,270 --> 00:01:44,771
Today,

56
00:01:44,771 --> 00:01:47,488
the historic city is only part of the

57
00:01:47,488 --> 00:01:49,204
municipality of Venice.

58
00:01:49,204 --> 00:01:52,278
Most of its residents actually live on the

59
00:01:52,278 --> 00:01:52,993
mainland,

60
00:01:52,993 --> 00:01:54,638
in places like Mestre,

61
00:01:54,638 --> 00:01:56,640
connected by a long bridge.

62
00:01:56,990 --> 00:01:58,147
It's beautiful.

63
00:01:58,147 --> 00:02:00,824
But think about how strange this is.

64
00:02:00,824 --> 00:02:03,211
The ground is soft mud and sand.

65
00:02:03,211 --> 00:02:04,658
The water is salty.

66
00:02:04,658 --> 00:02:05,598
Fresh water,

67
00:02:05,598 --> 00:02:08,203
stone and wood all had to come from

68
00:02:08,203 --> 00:02:09,360
somewhere else.

69
00:02:09,710 --> 00:02:12,640
No sensible planner would choose this place for

70
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:13,494
a great city.

71
00:02:13,494 --> 00:02:15,081
And nobody did choose it.

72
00:02:15,081 --> 00:02:16,790
People were forced into it.

73
00:02:19,740 --> 00:02:20,938
In Roman times,

74
00:02:20,938 --> 00:02:23,259
this region was rich and busy.

75
00:02:23,259 --> 00:02:25,805
The great city here was Aquileia,

76
00:02:25,805 --> 00:02:29,175
and nearby were towns like Padua and Altino.

77
00:02:29,175 --> 00:02:32,020
The lagoon was a place for fishermen.

78
00:02:32,370 --> 00:02:34,037
But in the four hundreds,

79
00:02:34,037 --> 00:02:36,859
the Western Roman Empire was falling apart,

80
00:02:36,859 --> 00:02:40,130
and invaders crossed the Alps into northern Italy.

81
00:02:40,480 --> 00:02:41,083
In 452,

82
00:02:41,083 --> 00:02:44,096
Attila and the Huns destroyed Aquileia.

83
00:02:44,096 --> 00:02:47,261
Many people from the mainland fled to the

84
00:02:47,261 --> 00:02:48,994
islands of the lagoon,

85
00:02:48,994 --> 00:02:51,480
where horsemen could not follow.

86
00:02:51,830 --> 00:02:54,656
Most went back when the danger passed.

87
00:02:54,656 --> 00:02:55,526
But in 568,

88
00:02:55,526 --> 00:02:58,352
the Lombards invaded Italy and stayed.

89
00:02:58,352 --> 00:02:59,149
This time,

90
00:02:59,149 --> 00:03:02,410
many refugees stayed in the lagoon for good.

91
00:03:02,760 --> 00:03:05,524
The lagoon was the perfect hiding place.

92
00:03:05,524 --> 00:03:08,154
The water is so shallow that big ships

93
00:03:08,154 --> 00:03:09,031
run aground,

94
00:03:09,031 --> 00:03:12,267
and the safe channels between the mudflats were

95
00:03:12,267 --> 00:03:14,020
known only to the locals.

96
00:03:14,370 --> 00:03:15,082
At first,

97
00:03:15,082 --> 00:03:18,213
the most important settlement was Torcello,

98
00:03:18,213 --> 00:03:20,917
an island in the north of the lagoon.

99
00:03:20,917 --> 00:03:23,977
Its cathedral was founded in the year 639,

100
00:03:23,977 --> 00:03:26,752
and a later version of it still stands

101
00:03:26,752 --> 00:03:27,250
today.

102
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:30,798
That defense was tested around the year 810,

103
00:03:30,798 --> 00:03:31,651
when Pepin,

104
00:03:31,651 --> 00:03:33,072
son of Charlemagne,

105
00:03:33,072 --> 00:03:35,276
attacked with a Frankish army.

106
00:03:35,276 --> 00:03:37,905
His fleet got stuck in the shallows,

107
00:03:37,905 --> 00:03:39,540
and the attack failed.

108
00:03:39,890 --> 00:03:40,672
Afterwards,

109
00:03:40,672 --> 00:03:43,930
the lagoon's government moved to the safest group

110
00:03:43,930 --> 00:03:45,624
of islands in the middle,

111
00:03:45,624 --> 00:03:47,774
around a place called Rivo Alto,

112
00:03:47,774 --> 00:03:48,751
the high bank.

113
00:03:48,751 --> 00:03:50,380
Today we call it Rialto.

114
00:03:50,730 --> 00:03:53,384
So Venice wasn't built on water in spite

115
00:03:53,384 --> 00:03:54,355
of the danger.

116
00:03:54,355 --> 00:03:56,751
It was built on water because of the

117
00:03:56,751 --> 00:03:57,268
danger.

118
00:03:57,268 --> 00:03:59,340
The lagoon was its castle wall.

119
00:03:59,690 --> 00:04:01,633
But how do you live in a swamp?

120
00:04:01,633 --> 00:04:04,487
The first lagoon people survived on two things

121
00:04:04,487 --> 00:04:06,308
the water gave them for free:

122
00:04:06,308 --> 00:04:06,673
fish,

123
00:04:06,673 --> 00:04:07,280
and salt.

124
00:04:07,630 --> 00:04:10,114
Fresh water was a constant problem.

125
00:04:10,114 --> 00:04:13,287
Venetians collected rain under their squares,

126
00:04:13,287 --> 00:04:16,944
filtering it through layers of sand into underground

127
00:04:16,944 --> 00:04:17,633
cisterns.

128
00:04:17,633 --> 00:04:20,600
Thousands of wellheads still dot the city.

129
00:04:20,950 --> 00:04:23,979
Salt was precious in a world without fridges,

130
00:04:23,979 --> 00:04:25,757
because it preserved food.

131
00:04:25,757 --> 00:04:28,853
Venetians harvested it in salt pans and traded

132
00:04:28,853 --> 00:04:31,223
it up the rivers of northern Italy.

133
00:04:31,223 --> 00:04:33,660
That was the start of their fortune.

134
00:04:36,610 --> 00:04:38,218
As the settlements grew,

135
00:04:38,218 --> 00:04:41,176
the Venetians had to solve a huge engineering

136
00:04:41,176 --> 00:04:41,755
problem.

137
00:04:41,755 --> 00:04:44,327
How do you put heavy stone buildings on

138
00:04:44,327 --> 00:04:44,970
soft mud?

139
00:04:45,320 --> 00:04:48,040
Their answer was to drive wooden piles deep

140
00:04:48,040 --> 00:04:48,905
into the mud,

141
00:04:48,905 --> 00:04:51,439
down to a firmer layer of compacted clay

142
00:04:51,439 --> 00:04:51,872
below.

143
00:04:51,872 --> 00:04:53,850
Then they built on top of them.

144
00:04:54,200 --> 00:04:56,839
The piles were packed tightly together,

145
00:04:56,839 --> 00:04:59,016
sometimes many per square meter.

146
00:04:59,016 --> 00:05:01,258
Workers hammered them in by hand,

147
00:05:01,258 --> 00:05:02,050
one by one.

148
00:05:02,400 --> 00:05:05,429
The great church of Santa Maria della Salute,

149
00:05:05,429 --> 00:05:06,944
finished in the 1680s,

150
00:05:06,944 --> 00:05:09,051
is said to stand on more than a

151
00:05:09,051 --> 00:05:10,500
million wooden piles.

152
00:05:10,850 --> 00:05:11,856
So in a sense,

153
00:05:11,856 --> 00:05:14,940
Venice stands on a forest turned upside down.

154
00:05:15,290 --> 00:05:17,131
The famous Rialto Bridge,

155
00:05:17,131 --> 00:05:20,460
finished in stone in 1591 after several wooden

156
00:05:20,460 --> 00:05:21,097
bridges,

157
00:05:21,097 --> 00:05:24,425
rests on thousands of wooden piles driven into

158
00:05:24,425 --> 00:05:26,550
the banks of the Grand Canal.

159
00:05:26,900 --> 00:05:29,472
You'd expect wood under water to rot.

160
00:05:29,472 --> 00:05:30,555
But it doesn't,

161
00:05:30,555 --> 00:05:33,127
because buried in mud and salt water,

162
00:05:33,127 --> 00:05:34,887
there's almost no oxygen.

163
00:05:34,887 --> 00:05:37,730
The microbes that rot wood can't survive.

164
00:05:38,080 --> 00:05:39,101
Over centuries,

165
00:05:39,101 --> 00:05:41,972
minerals from the water even seeped into the

166
00:05:41,972 --> 00:05:42,355
wood,

167
00:05:42,355 --> 00:05:43,504
making it harder.

168
00:05:43,504 --> 00:05:46,248
Some piles have held up buildings for many

169
00:05:46,248 --> 00:05:47,460
hundreds of years.

170
00:05:47,810 --> 00:05:50,213
When the bell tower of St.

171
00:05:50,213 --> 00:05:53,329
Mark's suddenly collapsed in 1902,

172
00:05:53,329 --> 00:05:56,800
the city rebuilt it exactly as before.

173
00:05:56,800 --> 00:05:58,135
The motto was:

174
00:05:58,135 --> 00:05:59,382
where it was,

175
00:05:59,382 --> 00:06:00,361
as it was.

176
00:06:00,361 --> 00:06:02,230
It reopened in 1912.

177
00:06:02,580 --> 00:06:04,987
On top of the piles came a platform

178
00:06:04,987 --> 00:06:05,589
of wood,

179
00:06:05,589 --> 00:06:07,930
then foundations of Istrian stone,

180
00:06:07,930 --> 00:06:10,939
a dense white limestone brought by ship from

181
00:06:10,939 --> 00:06:12,344
across the Adriatic.

182
00:06:12,344 --> 00:06:14,350
It barely lets water through.

183
00:06:14,700 --> 00:06:16,803
All that wood and stone had to come

184
00:06:16,803 --> 00:06:17,680
from far away.

185
00:06:17,680 --> 00:06:20,250
Timber was floated down the rivers from the

186
00:06:20,250 --> 00:06:22,412
forests of the Alps and the hills of

187
00:06:22,412 --> 00:06:23,230
the mainland.

188
00:06:23,580 --> 00:06:26,223
Venice protected its forests carefully,

189
00:06:26,223 --> 00:06:29,063
because without wood there would be no new

190
00:06:29,063 --> 00:06:29,790
buildings,

191
00:06:29,790 --> 00:06:30,715
and no ships.

192
00:06:30,715 --> 00:06:33,424
And ships were about to make Venice very

193
00:06:33,424 --> 00:06:33,820
rich.

194
00:06:36,770 --> 00:06:39,288
The lagoon gave Venice something else:

195
00:06:39,288 --> 00:06:41,225
there was almost no farmland.

196
00:06:41,225 --> 00:06:43,742
Venetians couldn't live from the land,

197
00:06:43,742 --> 00:06:45,550
so they lived from the sea.

198
00:06:45,900 --> 00:06:48,285
Venice sat at the northern tip of the

199
00:06:48,285 --> 00:06:48,912
Adriatic,

200
00:06:48,912 --> 00:06:51,422
the closest sea port to the rich cities

201
00:06:51,422 --> 00:06:52,615
of central Europe.

202
00:06:52,615 --> 00:06:55,313
Goods from the east could be shipped here,

203
00:06:55,313 --> 00:06:57,070
then carried over the Alps.

204
00:06:57,420 --> 00:06:58,058
In 1082,

205
00:06:58,058 --> 00:07:02,455
the Byzantine emperor gave Venetian merchants special trading

206
00:07:02,455 --> 00:07:04,299
rights across his empire,

207
00:07:04,299 --> 00:07:07,278
in exchange for help against his enemies.

208
00:07:07,278 --> 00:07:11,320
Venice's ships soon dominated trade with Constantinople.

209
00:07:11,670 --> 00:07:15,033
Venetian galleys sailed to Constantinople,

210
00:07:15,033 --> 00:07:16,910
to Alexandria in Egypt,

211
00:07:16,910 --> 00:07:19,412
and to the ports of the Levant,

212
00:07:19,412 --> 00:07:21,133
bringing back spices,

213
00:07:21,133 --> 00:07:21,602
silk,

214
00:07:21,602 --> 00:07:23,010
cotton and sugar.

215
00:07:23,360 --> 00:07:24,003
In 1204,

216
00:07:24,003 --> 00:07:27,574
Venice provided the ships for the Fourth Crusade,

217
00:07:27,574 --> 00:07:30,503
which ended up attacking Constantinople.

218
00:07:30,503 --> 00:07:33,431
Venice took a large share of the spoils,

219
00:07:33,431 --> 00:07:37,860
including ports and islands across the eastern Mediterranean.

220
00:07:38,210 --> 00:07:39,255
At its height,

221
00:07:39,255 --> 00:07:42,249
Venice ruled a chain of colonies along the

222
00:07:42,249 --> 00:07:43,573
coast of Dalmatia,

223
00:07:43,573 --> 00:07:44,339
in Greece,

224
00:07:44,339 --> 00:07:47,403
and on large islands like Crete and Cyprus.

225
00:07:47,403 --> 00:07:49,980
A maritime empire run from a lagoon.

226
00:07:50,330 --> 00:07:52,726
Its ships came from the Arsenal,

227
00:07:52,726 --> 00:07:54,469
a giant state shipyard.

228
00:07:54,469 --> 00:07:57,810
Thousands of workers built galleys in stages,

229
00:07:57,810 --> 00:07:59,988
almost like an assembly line,

230
00:07:59,988 --> 00:08:02,530
centuries before modern factories.

231
00:08:02,880 --> 00:08:03,536
In 1574,

232
00:08:03,536 --> 00:08:06,450
to impress the visiting king of France,

233
00:08:06,450 --> 00:08:09,363
the Arsenal is said to have assembled a

234
00:08:09,363 --> 00:08:12,569
complete galley during the time he spent at

235
00:08:12,569 --> 00:08:13,370
a banquet.

236
00:08:13,720 --> 00:08:15,343
Venetians traveled far.

237
00:08:15,343 --> 00:08:16,154
Marco Polo,

238
00:08:16,154 --> 00:08:17,574
a Venetian merchant,

239
00:08:17,574 --> 00:08:20,819
journeyed to China in the late twelve hundreds,

240
00:08:20,819 --> 00:08:23,388
and his book made Europe dream of the

241
00:08:23,388 --> 00:08:24,470
riches of Asia.

242
00:08:24,820 --> 00:08:27,191
To protect the city from fire,

243
00:08:27,191 --> 00:08:30,787
glassmakers were moved to the island of Murano

244
00:08:30,787 --> 00:08:31,475
in 1291.

245
00:08:31,475 --> 00:08:35,070
Their techniques were closely guarded secrets,

246
00:08:35,070 --> 00:08:38,130
and Murano glass is still famous today.

247
00:08:38,480 --> 00:08:40,383
Trade also brought disease.

248
00:08:40,383 --> 00:08:43,238
Venice set up some of the first permanent

249
00:08:43,238 --> 00:08:46,364
quarantine stations on islands in the lagoon.

250
00:08:46,364 --> 00:08:48,675
Ships had to wait there for up to

251
00:08:48,675 --> 00:08:49,491
forty days,

252
00:08:49,491 --> 00:08:50,375
in Venetian,

253
00:08:50,375 --> 00:08:51,530
quaranta giorni.

254
00:08:51,880 --> 00:08:52,837
Around 1500,

255
00:08:52,837 --> 00:08:56,077
Venice was also one of the leading printing

256
00:08:56,077 --> 00:08:57,476
centers in Europe.

257
00:08:57,476 --> 00:09:01,526
Printers like Aldus Manutius published Greek and Latin

258
00:09:01,526 --> 00:09:02,925
classics in small,

259
00:09:02,925 --> 00:09:04,250
affordable books.

260
00:09:04,600 --> 00:09:06,546
The city was also a republic,

261
00:09:06,546 --> 00:09:09,335
ruled by an elected leader called the doge

262
00:09:09,335 --> 00:09:11,605
and a council of wealthy families.

263
00:09:11,605 --> 00:09:14,070
It lasted for about a thousand years.

264
00:09:14,420 --> 00:09:15,301
Every year,

265
00:09:15,301 --> 00:09:16,621
on Ascension Day,

266
00:09:16,621 --> 00:09:19,263
the doge sailed out to the open sea

267
00:09:19,263 --> 00:09:21,758
in a golden ship and threw a ring

268
00:09:21,758 --> 00:09:22,932
into the water,

269
00:09:22,932 --> 00:09:25,940
symbolically marrying Venice to the sea.

270
00:09:26,290 --> 00:09:29,223
With the money came the palaces along the

271
00:09:29,223 --> 00:09:30,131
Grand Canal,

272
00:09:30,131 --> 00:09:33,205
the Doge's Palace and the golden mosaics of

273
00:09:33,205 --> 00:09:33,484
St.

274
00:09:33,484 --> 00:09:34,671
Mark's Basilica.

275
00:09:34,671 --> 00:09:35,579
Around 1500,

276
00:09:35,579 --> 00:09:38,513
Venice was one of the biggest and richest

277
00:09:38,513 --> 00:09:39,770
cities in Europe.

278
00:09:40,120 --> 00:09:41,646
If you're enjoying this,

279
00:09:41,646 --> 00:09:42,317
subscribe.

280
00:09:42,317 --> 00:09:44,697
Every week we find the story hidden in

281
00:09:44,697 --> 00:09:45,490
another map.

282
00:09:48,440 --> 00:09:50,845
But there was a hidden problem.

283
00:09:50,845 --> 00:09:52,875
A lagoon is not permanent.

284
00:09:52,875 --> 00:09:53,776
Left alone,

285
00:09:53,776 --> 00:09:55,430
it slowly disappears.

286
00:09:55,780 --> 00:09:57,723
Rivers bring mud and sand,

287
00:09:57,723 --> 00:10:00,888
which slowly fill the lagoon until it turns

288
00:10:00,888 --> 00:10:02,687
into marsh and dry land.

289
00:10:02,687 --> 00:10:03,334
The sea,

290
00:10:03,334 --> 00:10:04,701
on the other hand,

291
00:10:04,701 --> 00:10:07,795
can break through the sandbars and turn it

292
00:10:07,795 --> 00:10:09,090
into an open bay.

293
00:10:09,440 --> 00:10:10,303
Either way,

294
00:10:10,303 --> 00:10:13,182
Venice would lose its protective water.

295
00:10:13,182 --> 00:10:14,477
So for centuries,

296
00:10:14,477 --> 00:10:17,859
the Republic fought to keep the lagoon exactly

297
00:10:17,859 --> 00:10:18,650
as it was.

298
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:20,739
In the 1500s and 1600s,

299
00:10:20,739 --> 00:10:24,363
Venetian engineers dug new channels to divert the

300
00:10:24,363 --> 00:10:25,450
rivers Brenta,

301
00:10:25,450 --> 00:10:28,132
Piave and Sile away from the lagoon,

302
00:10:28,132 --> 00:10:31,248
so that their mud would flow straight into

303
00:10:31,248 --> 00:10:32,480
the sea instead.

304
00:10:32,830 --> 00:10:33,461
In 1604,

305
00:10:33,461 --> 00:10:36,056
they even moved part of the mouth of

306
00:10:36,056 --> 00:10:36,617
the Po,

307
00:10:36,617 --> 00:10:38,231
Italy's longest river,

308
00:10:38,231 --> 00:10:39,283
further south,

309
00:10:39,283 --> 00:10:42,509
to stop its sediment from drifting toward the

310
00:10:42,509 --> 00:10:43,070
lagoon.

311
00:10:43,420 --> 00:10:44,485
In the 1700s,

312
00:10:44,485 --> 00:10:46,311
they built the murazzi,

313
00:10:46,311 --> 00:10:50,039
massive stone sea walls along the thin sandbars,

314
00:10:50,039 --> 00:10:53,310
to stop storm waves from breaking through.

315
00:10:53,660 --> 00:10:56,493
A special magistrate for the waters watched over

316
00:10:56,493 --> 00:10:57,534
the whole system.

317
00:10:57,534 --> 00:11:00,310
Damaging the lagoon could be punished severely.

318
00:11:00,660 --> 00:11:03,296
So the lagoon you see today is not

319
00:11:03,296 --> 00:11:04,652
entirely natural.

320
00:11:04,652 --> 00:11:07,890
It has been shaped and protected by people

321
00:11:07,890 --> 00:11:09,020
for centuries.

322
00:11:09,370 --> 00:11:10,206
Meanwhile,

323
00:11:10,206 --> 00:11:12,030
the world was changing.

324
00:11:12,030 --> 00:11:12,941
After 1498,

325
00:11:12,941 --> 00:11:16,969
when Portuguese sailors reached India around Africa,

326
00:11:16,969 --> 00:11:19,780
more of the spice trade moved to the

327
00:11:19,780 --> 00:11:20,540
Atlantic.

328
00:11:20,890 --> 00:11:23,349
Venice slowly lost its power.

329
00:11:23,349 --> 00:11:24,086
In 1797,

330
00:11:24,086 --> 00:11:26,135
Napoleon's army arrived,

331
00:11:26,135 --> 00:11:27,610
and the Republic,

332
00:11:27,610 --> 00:11:30,069
after about a thousand years,

333
00:11:30,069 --> 00:11:31,380
came to an end.

334
00:11:31,730 --> 00:11:34,922
Napoleon even had the bronze horses of St.

335
00:11:34,922 --> 00:11:36,629
Mark's taken to Paris.

336
00:11:36,629 --> 00:11:39,524
They only came back to Venice in 1815,

337
00:11:39,524 --> 00:11:40,860
after his defeat.

338
00:11:41,210 --> 00:11:41,819
Later,

339
00:11:41,819 --> 00:11:45,388
Venice was finally tied to the mainland.

340
00:11:45,388 --> 00:11:48,261
A railway bridge opened in 1846,

341
00:11:48,261 --> 00:11:50,611
and a road bridge in 1933.

342
00:11:50,611 --> 00:11:54,180
The island city was no longer an island.

343
00:11:57,130 --> 00:11:57,686
Today,

344
00:11:57,686 --> 00:12:00,941
Venice faces a new version of its oldest

345
00:12:00,941 --> 00:12:01,497
enemy.

346
00:12:01,497 --> 00:12:03,165
The water is rising,

347
00:12:03,165 --> 00:12:05,150
and the city is sinking.

348
00:12:05,500 --> 00:12:07,134
Several times a year,

349
00:12:07,134 --> 00:12:09,881
high tides push water into the city.

350
00:12:09,881 --> 00:12:12,109
Venetians call it acqua alta,

351
00:12:12,109 --> 00:12:13,000
high water.

352
00:12:13,350 --> 00:12:16,419
It happens when several things combine:

353
00:12:16,419 --> 00:12:17,417
a high tide,

354
00:12:17,417 --> 00:12:18,798
low air pressure,

355
00:12:18,798 --> 00:12:21,100
and a strong south-east wind,

356
00:12:21,100 --> 00:12:22,174
the scirocco,

357
00:12:22,174 --> 00:12:24,246
pushing water up the long,

358
00:12:24,246 --> 00:12:25,550
narrow Adriatic.

359
00:12:25,900 --> 00:12:26,147
St.

360
00:12:26,147 --> 00:12:28,682
Mark's Square is one of the lowest parts

361
00:12:28,682 --> 00:12:29,486
of the city,

362
00:12:29,486 --> 00:12:31,588
so it is often the first place to

363
00:12:31,588 --> 00:12:32,021
flood.

364
00:12:32,021 --> 00:12:34,680
People cross it on raised wooden walkways.

365
00:12:35,030 --> 00:12:37,972
The worst flood on record came in November

366
00:12:37,972 --> 00:12:38,383
1966,

367
00:12:38,383 --> 00:12:41,462
when the water reached about one hundred and

368
00:12:41,462 --> 00:12:44,062
ninety-four centimeters above normal.

369
00:12:44,062 --> 00:12:45,294
In November 2019,

370
00:12:45,294 --> 00:12:48,510
it reached about one hundred and eighty-seven.

371
00:12:48,860 --> 00:12:50,051
At the same time,

372
00:12:50,051 --> 00:12:51,838
the land has been sinking.

373
00:12:51,838 --> 00:12:53,558
In the twentieth century,

374
00:12:53,558 --> 00:12:57,132
pumping groundwater for industry on the mainland made

375
00:12:57,132 --> 00:12:58,455
the ground compact.

376
00:12:58,455 --> 00:13:00,970
The pumping was stopped in the 1970s.

377
00:13:01,320 --> 00:13:02,993
Add rising sea levels,

378
00:13:02,993 --> 00:13:06,922
and Venice has lost around twenty-five centimeters of

379
00:13:06,922 --> 00:13:09,760
height relative to the sea since 1900.

380
00:13:10,110 --> 00:13:12,843
So Italy built a new kind of defense,

381
00:13:12,843 --> 00:13:15,719
at the same three inlets where the tide

382
00:13:15,719 --> 00:13:17,086
enters the lagoon.

383
00:13:17,086 --> 00:13:18,380
It's called MOSE.

384
00:13:18,730 --> 00:13:22,344
MOSE is made of seventy-eight steel gates lying

385
00:13:22,344 --> 00:13:23,474
on the seabed.

386
00:13:23,474 --> 00:13:26,109
When a dangerous tide is forecast,

387
00:13:26,109 --> 00:13:27,464
air is pumped in,

388
00:13:27,464 --> 00:13:30,250
and the gates rise to block the sea.

389
00:13:30,600 --> 00:13:33,915
The project took decades and cost billions of

390
00:13:33,915 --> 00:13:34,419
euros.

391
00:13:34,419 --> 00:13:37,374
The gates were raised for the first time

392
00:13:37,374 --> 00:13:38,599
in October 2020,

393
00:13:38,599 --> 00:13:39,608
and that day,

394
00:13:39,608 --> 00:13:39,896
St.

395
00:13:39,896 --> 00:13:41,770
Mark's Square stayed dry.

396
00:13:42,120 --> 00:13:43,512
But there's a catch.

397
00:13:43,512 --> 00:13:45,302
Each time the gates close,

398
00:13:45,302 --> 00:13:47,688
the lagoon is cut off from the sea.

399
00:13:47,688 --> 00:13:49,478
If sea levels keep rising,

400
00:13:49,478 --> 00:13:51,930
they may need to close so often that

401
00:13:51,930 --> 00:13:53,720
the lagoon itself suffers.

402
00:13:56,670 --> 00:13:58,246
There's another threat,

403
00:13:58,246 --> 00:13:59,691
and it's not the sea.

404
00:13:59,691 --> 00:14:00,610
In the 1950s,

405
00:14:00,610 --> 00:14:04,420
around one hundred and seventy-five thousand people lived

406
00:14:04,420 --> 00:14:05,864
in the historic city.

407
00:14:05,864 --> 00:14:08,360
Today it's fewer than fifty thousand.

408
00:14:08,710 --> 00:14:11,990
Living on the water is expensive and difficult.

409
00:14:11,990 --> 00:14:13,903
Homes are hard to maintain,

410
00:14:13,903 --> 00:14:16,362
everything must be carried by boat,

411
00:14:16,362 --> 00:14:19,710
and many apartments have become holiday rentals.

412
00:14:20,060 --> 00:14:20,909
Meanwhile,

413
00:14:20,909 --> 00:14:23,920
millions of tourists visit every year,

414
00:14:23,920 --> 00:14:25,696
many only for the day.

415
00:14:25,696 --> 00:14:26,391
In 2024,

416
00:14:26,391 --> 00:14:30,405
Venice became the first city to charge day-trippers

417
00:14:30,405 --> 00:14:33,030
an entry fee on its busiest days.

418
00:14:33,380 --> 00:14:34,310
In the 1500s,

419
00:14:34,310 --> 00:14:37,765
thousands of gondolas moved people around the city.

420
00:14:37,765 --> 00:14:40,156
Today there are only a few hundred,

421
00:14:40,156 --> 00:14:41,884
mostly carrying visitors.

422
00:14:41,884 --> 00:14:44,475
Each one is built slightly asymmetric,

423
00:14:44,475 --> 00:14:47,198
so that a single oarsman standing on one

424
00:14:47,198 --> 00:14:49,590
side can row it in a straight line.

425
00:14:49,940 --> 00:14:50,500
And yet,

426
00:14:50,500 --> 00:14:51,745
for those who stay,

427
00:14:51,745 --> 00:14:53,612
Venice offers something rare:

428
00:14:53,612 --> 00:14:56,289
it's one of the largest car-free cities in

429
00:14:56,289 --> 00:14:56,974
the world.

430
00:14:56,974 --> 00:14:59,339
Children walk to school over bridges,

431
00:14:59,339 --> 00:15:01,642
and the only traffic jams are on the

432
00:15:01,642 --> 00:15:02,140
canals.

433
00:15:02,490 --> 00:15:03,834
Huge cruise ships,

434
00:15:03,834 --> 00:15:06,452
once a common sight sailing past St.

435
00:15:06,452 --> 00:15:07,018
Mark's,

436
00:15:07,018 --> 00:15:09,990
were banned from the city center in 2021.

437
00:15:10,340 --> 00:15:11,237
Since 1987,

438
00:15:11,237 --> 00:15:14,300
Venice and its lagoon have been a UNESCO

439
00:15:14,300 --> 00:15:15,869
World Heritage Site.

440
00:15:15,869 --> 00:15:17,662
Not just the buildings,

441
00:15:17,662 --> 00:15:19,380
but the lagoon itself.

442
00:15:19,730 --> 00:15:22,437
Because that's the real lesson of Venice.

443
00:15:22,437 --> 00:15:25,014
The city and the water are not enemies.

444
00:15:25,014 --> 00:15:26,303
Without the lagoon,

445
00:15:26,303 --> 00:15:28,430
Venice would never have existed.

446
00:15:28,780 --> 00:15:30,990
So let's put the whole story together.

447
00:15:31,340 --> 00:15:34,455
Venice was built on water because the lagoon

448
00:15:34,455 --> 00:15:37,431
was the safest place in a dangerous world.

449
00:15:37,431 --> 00:15:39,024
Invaders on horseback,

450
00:15:39,024 --> 00:15:40,616
and even large fleets,

451
00:15:40,616 --> 00:15:42,000
could not reach it.

452
00:15:42,350 --> 00:15:45,604
Millions of wooden piles made it possible to

453
00:15:45,604 --> 00:15:46,762
build in stone.

454
00:15:46,762 --> 00:15:48,353
And with no farmland,

455
00:15:48,353 --> 00:15:51,390
the sea became the city's road to wealth.

456
00:15:51,740 --> 00:15:53,330
For fifteen hundred years,

457
00:15:53,330 --> 00:15:56,511
Venice has survived by understanding the water around

458
00:15:56,511 --> 00:15:56,747
it.

459
00:15:56,747 --> 00:15:59,574
Whether it survives the next century depends on

460
00:15:59,574 --> 00:16:00,870
whether we still can.

461
00:16:01,220 --> 00:16:05,506
Every map hides a story like this one.

462
00:16:05,506 --> 00:16:09,683
If you want to discover the next one,

463
00:16:09,683 --> 00:16:12,870
subscribe to Timeline Earth.
